# PART 2 — SUMMARY OF ASSISTANT ANALYSIS

What follows is a condensed summary of the analysis provided in response to the above evidence, so
the reasoning chain survives without the full prose.

## 2.1 Identification of the app

- The app is **`fserver/client/game.html`** — the only project with a "Graphics" High/Low setting
  (`data-gfx="high"/"low"`, `setLowGfx`→`html.gfx-low`).
- `images/mapped_from_owner` under `pid=9792` does NOT come from this app: fserver has zero `<img>`
  anywhere (only `net.js:102` matches "img" and it is unrelated). pid 9792 is another open content
  process/page; its decoded bitmaps (365x650 / 365x649 / 366x512 card-sized) appeared in every
  snapshot and grew (17→29→31 MB), then fell to 1.38 MB after a memory purge. Do not attribute
  these to the game.

## 2.2 What the snapshots showed (notation: S1 = msg1, S2 = msg2, S3 = msg3, S4 = msg6)

Key GPU-process figures:

| metric | S1 | S2 | S3 (swgl) | S4 (after minimize) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| explicit total | 37.5 MB | 35.1 MB | 340.4 MB | 292.3 MB |
| swgl | — | — | 250.8 MB | 257.3 MB |
| render-targets | 120 MB | 192 MB | 144 MB | 141 MB |
| texture-cache (atlas) | 41.4 (36) | 38.5 (38.5) | 37.5 (37.5) | 39.5 (39.5) |
| depth-targets | 17.1 | 34.0 | 32.0 | 34.5 |
| render-texture-hosts | 20.4 | 32.2 | 32.8 | (merged/tiny) |
| swap-chains | 27.2 | 27.3 | 27.6 | 27.6 |
| picture-tiles | 24.1 | 26.0 | — | — |
| gpu-committed | 2,575 | 6,742 | 0 | 0 |
| gpu-shared | 2,614 | 6,655 | 0 | 0 |
| resident | 2,767 | 6,845 | 16,115 | 16,076 |
| private | 2,752 | 6,974 | 16,526 | 16,484 |
| commit | 3,444 | 7,670 | 17,219 | 17,121 |
| heap-allocated | 35.9 | 33.8 | 338.8 | 290.7 |
| images pid 9792 | 17.4 | 29.2 | 29.2 | ~1.4 |

Reading:
- S1→S2: the **growing buckets** are the ones behind continuous SVG re-rasterization:
  render-targets 120→192 (+72), depth-targets 17→34 (+17), render-texture-hosts 20→32 (+12);
  atlas is essentially flat. gpu-shared/committed ballooned 2.6→6.7 GB. So the app **grows over
  time during animated play**.
- S3: **renderer fell back to Software WebRender (swgl)** inside the SAME GPU process (same pid
  15100): all `gpu-*` counters → 0, explicit `swgl` bucket 250.8 MB, system heap-allocated
  34→339 MB, and resident/private/commit exploded to ~16-17 GB.
- S4: "Minimize memory usage" (GC/CC/purge) shrank everything Firefox attributes
  (heap-unclassified 80→25 MB, mapped images 31→1.4 MB) but the ~16 GB ghost did not move at
  all. Conclusion: the ghost is **not under about:memory's jurisdiction** — it is private,
  committed, resident virtual memory that no Firefox memory reporter can see, allocate, or free.

## 2.3 Root causes in the app code (addressable pool = the explicit ~245-260 MB)

1. **Hull connectors animate forever.** `renderConnections`/`drawConnGlow`
   (game.html:1470-1479) plus CSS `.conn-base` (`conn-march` 2.4s) and `.conn-pulse`
   (`conn-pulse-flow` 3.2s) at game.html:69-72: infinite `stroke-dashoffset` animations on every
   orthogonally adjacent hull pair, re-rasterized by WebRender every frame, even when the board is
   idle. This is the largest single feed into render-targets / render-texture-hosts /
   upload-staging.
2. **Always-on heavy filters.** `.piece .module-svg` carries a double `drop-shadow`
   (game.html:87-89) per piece → one filter surface per piece retained in the texture-cache atlas
   (~36-40 MB), rebuilt every frame the filtered pixels change (the per-piece inner animations).
3. **Too many simultaneous inner animations per piece** (pieces.js): triangle 14s + 6s
   counter-rotate; circle 9s + 6.5s counter-rotate; diamond 12s rotate + shimmer; square 4 blink
   LEDs + scan + shimmer; core-pulse on hex/circle (2.6s); ping on hex. Each is an animated
   sub-group → more rasterized sub-groups changing per frame.
4. **No invalidation isolation.** All pieces live in one shared `.grid` transform layer; a dirty
   animated sub-element can invalidate larger board tiles than its own box → picture-tiles pool
   (24-26 MB).
5. Bounded/transient animations (route `.move-path` `path-flow`/`path-pulse-flow`, ping,
   blockflash, found-pulse) only run while hovering/acting — kept.

Note: the board uses CSS keyframes (moved off SMIL deliberately per pieces.js:36-40) precisely to
avoid SMIL leak-on-remove, which previously leaked memory every time a piece node was dropped.

## 2.4 The driver failure (decisive evidence, msg 8)

`about:support` failure log decoded:
- `Present1 failed: 0x887a0005 reason 0x887a0020` = DXGI_ERROR_DEVICE_REMOVED, reason
  **DRIVER_INTERNAL_ERROR**, occurring at `WR_POST_UPDATE` (while composing/presenting).
- Then `Handle DeviceReset`, `Crash during D3D11 device creation`, `Detect DeviceReset ...
  DRIVER_ERROR`, `Failed to connect WebRenderBridgeChild`, `Fallback WR to SW-WR`,
  `RcANGLE(no compositor device)`, `CompositorDevice does not exist`.
- Meaning: the D3D11 device was removed by a driver internal error, Firefox could not recreate a
  device (creation itself crashed), and fell back to software WebRender **in the same GPU
  process** (which is why pid never changed and gpu-* counters went to 0 while swgl appeared).
- **No Event ID 4101 does not contradict this**: 4101 is the WDDM TDR-timeout event; the
  DXGI_ERROR_DRIVER_INTERNAL_ERROR device-removal path frequently concludes without a TDR event.
  The about:support failure log IS the reset record.

## 2.5 The full causal chain (final understanding)

1. Game on High produces sustained, never-idle compositor/present load (per-frame SVG raster,
   filters, connectors, many animated sub-groups) on a marginal D3D11 driver.
2. This reliably trips `Present1 ... DRIVER_INTERNAL_ERROR` over sustained play (reproducible
   every session of the game).
3. Device reset → device re-creation crash → **Fallback WR to SW-WR** in-process.
4. The failed recreation leaves a ~15-16 GB private/commit allocation attributed to the GPU
   process that no Firefox reporter can purge (the "ghost").
5. Therefore: **the game is the trigger; the 16 GB is the fallout.** App-side High→Low or
   about:memory Minimize cannot undo step 4 once it has happened (which is why msg 5 and msg 6
   showed it "unaffected").

## 2.6 Cold-start experiments (msg 10) proved the High layer leaks GPU memory

| scenario | ~30 s | 1.5 min | 3 min | 5 min | failures |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| about:memory only | 281 MB | — | — | — | none |
| github + simple JS | 300 MB | — | — | — | none |
| Game, Low | 700 | 682 | — | 658 | none |
| Game, High | 954 | 900 | 3,400 | 4,000 | none |

- **Low: flat** (~658-700 MB, no growth).
- **High: monotonically growing** (~0.9 → 4.0 GB in 5 min, burst between 1.5-3 min). Extended, this
  is exactly the accumulation that reached 16 GB and tripped the device error / swgl fallback.
- Conclusion: the High-detail animation layer **leaks/accumulates GPU-process memory monotonically**;
  the Low tier does not reproduce it. Correlation confirmed and app-attributable to the High
  animation rendering.

## 2.7 Plan of fixes (Phases, measurement-first)

Pass criterion: cold-start High stops growing — stays under ~1.2 GB total with <200 MB growth over
5 min; Low unchanged (~660-700 MB).

- **Phase 0 — baseline protocol.** Fresh Firefox; cold-start game on High; sample about:memory GPU
  at 30s/1.5min/3min/5min (user already has the numbers). Also take a full save-to-file snapshot
  at 1 min and 4 min on High to identify the *growing* bucket (gfx/webrender/textures subtrees vs
  gpu-shared/gpu-committed) so Phases target the right pool.
- **Phase 1 — kill idle connector raster.** `drawConnGlow` (game.html:1470-1479): make `conn-base`
  a static dashed line (remove `conn-march`, :69-70); keep a single `conn-pulse` line gated to
  active play (add a grid/root class while `G.pending`/telegraph/move is in flight, mirroring the
  `gfx-low` idiom). Board that is still = genuinely static in the compositor. Biggest expected win
  on render-targets/render-texture-hosts.
- **Phase 2 — filters only when they earn it.** Base `.piece .module-svg` double drop-shadow
  (game.html:87-89) → none; move the glow to state classes only (`.sel`, `.ready`, `.moving`,
  `.targetable`, `.found`, `.dep` when active; keep `.ast` saturate). Removes per-piece filter
  surfaces and per-frame filter rebuild churn (texture-cache atlas + depth-targets).
- **Phase 3 — trim redundant per-piece motion at High.** Drop the second counter-rotate on
  triangle/circle/diamond and the blink/scan/shimmer groups (pieces.js); keep one signature
  rotation + `core-pulse`/`ping` (opacity/scale animate on the compositor, near-free).
- **Phase 4 — isolate invalidation.** `contain: layout style` on `.piece` (NOT `paint` — paint
  clips asteroids' floating companions and scaled deployables). Measure; revert if no win.
- **Phase 5 — conditional.** If Phases 1-4 do not flatten the High growth, the remaining
  accumulation is a Firefox WebRender texture-cache/pool eviction issue → build a minimal repro +
  keep the failure log, file a Mozilla bug; app fallback = a "Medium" tier that reuses Low's
  connector behavior.
- **Verification of invariants.** `renderConnections` restarts animations on rebuild and
  `paintPieces` reuses nodes so rotations continue; `setLowGfx` rebuilds the board. Changes must
  preserve the diff-reuse behavior (game.html:1503-1506, 1661-1688, 1433-1442).

## 2.8 Environment / out-of-scope items

- The ~16 GB ghost is bound to the *broken device session*; a fresh Firefox restart should restore
  a sane GPU baseline. If it reproduces only after the failure recurs, driver/platform fault is the
  root and the end state is a driver update and/or a Mozilla bug report (grab the bp-ID from
  `about:crashes` for the device-creation crash).
- Whether the machine is a VM / remote-desktop session is **still unanswered** — RDP/VM setups hit
  DRIVER_INTERNAL_ERROR routinely (no real D3D adapter) and would make swgl the permanent end
  state for any heavy page.
- `gpu-committed`/`gpu-shared`/`resident` are OS/commit counters; the physical, reusable store the
  app can influence is the WebRender texture pool (~245-260 MB explicit). WebRender holds it until
  memory pressure — fixes target the pool and the per-frame churn feeding it.

## 2.9 Open items / next actions

- Confirm machine is VM/RDP or not.
- Capture the 1-min/4-min full snapshot on High to identify the growing bucket (recommended before
  coding so Phases target the right pool).
- Update GPU driver; check `about:crashes` for a bp-ID.
- Implement Phases 1-4 after plan approval; re-run the cold-start 5-min High test after each phase
  to verify the growth flattens; keep Low as the control.

